NAME
    load - write a raw file's bytes into memory

SYNOPSIS
    load <space> <addr> <file>

DESCRIPTION
    Reads home/Logs/<CoreName>/<file> (typically
    one 'dump' produced, or hand-edited afterward) and pokes its raw bytes
    into <space> starting at
    <addr>. Refuses if <space> isn't writable.

    The file name is taken from home/Logs/<CoreName>/: a bare name or one
    with '../' in it is resolved from there, one with a leading '/' from
    this shell's root, as every other command reads a path (see 'man
    hier'). A name that lands above the root is refused, so '../../../x'
    and an absolute path of the machine cannot write or read outside it.
    Before 2026-10-10 the name was joined to the folder unchecked, and
    both did.

EXAMPLES
    load WRAM 0 wram.bin
    load WRAM 0 /tmp/wram.bin
